{UTF-8}
Endogenous Purchase Rate=
	0
	~	Units/Year
	~	The endogenous flow of units purchased by the adopters.  You will need to \
		enter the structure to capture the replacement of units being retired and \
		the adjustment of the installed base to the desired value determined by \
		the number of adopters and the desired number of units per adopter.
	|

Net Population Increase=
	0
	~	Households/Year
	~	The net increase in the number of households is set to zero for testing \
		purposes.
	|

Purchase Rate=
	Switch for Exogenous Purchase Rate*Exogenous Purchases + (1-Switch for Exogenous Purchase Rate\
		)*Endogenous Purchase Rate
	~	Units/Year
	~	For testing purposes, the purchase rate is set either to an exogenous rate \
		or the endogenous rate.
	|

Exogenous Purchases=
	Step(Change in Purchase Rate, Time to Change Purchase Rate)
	~	Units/Year
	~	For testing purposes, the exogenous purchase rate follows a STEP input:  \
		it is zero until the Time to Change Purchase Rate, and then jumps \
		sudeently to the Change in Purchase Rate.
	|

Switch for Exogenous Purchase Rate=
	1
	~	Dimensionless [0,1,1]
	~	When the Switch = 1, the purchase rate is exogenous; Switch = 0 means the \
		purchase rate is determined by endogenous purchases.
	|

Population Willing to Adopt M=
	Fraction Willing to Adopt*Total Population N
	~	Household
	~	The population willing to adopt the product is given by the fraction \
		willing to adopt and the total population.  The fraction willing to adopt \
		includes those who wish to adopt at current prices and product attributes \
		and those who already have adopted.
	|

Price=
	Switch for Exogenous Price*Exogenous Price for Testing + (1-Switch for Exogenous Price\
		)*Average Price
	~	$/unit
	~		|

Initial Exogenous Purchase Rate=
	10000
	~	Units/Year
	~	The exogenous initial rate of purchases, used in partial model testing.
	|

Exogenous Fractional Growth in Purchases=
	0
	~	1/Year
	~	The fractional growth in exogenous purchases, used in partial model \
		testing.
	|

Exogenous Price for Testing=
	2500
	~	$/unit [0,2500]
	~	The exogenous price of the product for purposes of testing the response of \
		the adoption sector to price, through the fraction willing to adopt.
	|

Exogenous Purchase Rate=
	Initial Exogenous Purchase Rate*exp(Exogenous Fractional Growth in Purchases*(Time -\
		 INITIAL TIME))
	~	Units/Year
	~	For partial model testing of the learning curve, the exogenous purchase \
		rate grows exponentially at a specified rate from the intial exogenous \
		value.
	|

Switch for Exogenous Purchases=
	1
	~	Dimensionless [0,1,1]
	~	Switch to determine whether production experience is determined by the \
		exogenous purchase rate (for partial model testing; when the switch = 1), \
		or by the endogenous purchase rate (for the full model; when the Switch = \
		0).
	|

Switch for Exogenous Price=
	1
	~	Dimensionless [0,1,1]
	~	Switch indicating whether the fraction willing to adopt is determined by \
		the exogenous price (for partial model testing) or by the average price \
		determined endogenously by the learning curve and cumulative production \
		experience.  Switch = 1 -> exogenous price; 0 -> endogenous price.
	|

Change in Purchase Rate=
	1e+07
	~	Units/Year
	~	For testing purposes the desired number of units will jump by this amount \
		at the specified time.
	|

Time to Change Purchase Rate=
	1980
	~	Year
	~	For testing purposes the desired number of units will increase in the year \
		1980 from zero to a new level.
	|

Contacts with Adopters CA=
	Probability of Contact with Adopters PCA*Social Contacts SC
	~	Households/Year
	~	The total number of contacts the potential adopters have with adopters \
		each year.  Determined by the total contact volume, given by Social \
		Contacts SC, and the probability that any such contact occurs with an \
		adopter.
	|

Probability of Contact with Adopters PCA=
	Adopters A/Total Population N
	~	Dimensionless
	~	In the Bass model, we assume that the population is well mixed, so that \
		the probability of contacting an adopter is simply the proportion of \
		adopters in the total population.
	|

Initial Installed Base=
	0
	~	Units [0,1e+06,1000]
	~	The initial number of product in use.
	|

Installed Base= INTEG (
	Purchase Rate-Product Retirement,
		Initial Installed Base)
	~	Units
	~	The installed base of the product is the accumulation of purchases less \
		product retirement.
	|

Initial Adopters A0=
	0
	~	Households [0,1e+06,1000]
	~	The initial number of adopters in the population.
	|

Social Contacts SC=
	Contact Rate c*Potential Adopters P
	~	Household/Year
	~	The total number of contacts generated by the potential adopters each year \
		is determined by the size of the potential adopter population and the \
		average number of contacts each person has per year (the Contact Rate, c).
	|

Startup Costs=
	Unit Cost*Initial Installed Base
	~	$
	~	The cost of seeding the market with the initial installed base, at \
		manufacturing cost.  This up-front development cost is deducted from \
		cumulative profit.
	|

Revenue=
	Average Price * Purchase Rate
	~	$/Year
	~	Revenue is the product of the price and purchase rate.
	|

Net Income=
	Gross Profit - Fixed Costs
	~	$/Year
	~	Net income is gross profit less fixed costs.
	|

Cost of Goods Sold=
	Unit Cost* Purchase Rate
	~	$/Year
	~	The cost of goods sold (variable costs) depend on unit costs and the sales \
		rate.
	|

Cumulative Profit= INTEG (
	Net Income,
		-Startup Costs)
	~	$
	~	Cumulative profit.  Initialized equal to the (negative of) startup costs \
		(total cost incurred prior to product launch, assumed to be the value of \
		the initial installed base).
	|

Fixed Costs=
	5e+07
	~	$/Year
	~	Fixed costs include G&A expense, product development costs and other fixed \
		costs attributed to the product.
	|

Gross Profit=
	Revenue - Cost of Goods Sold
	~	$/Year
	~	Gross profit is revenue less the cost of goods sold.
	|

Product Retirement=
	DELAY N(Purchase Rate, Useful Life of Product, 0, 6)
	~	Units/Year
	~	The rate at which units of the product in use is discarded due to \
		failures, obsolescence, and so on.  Modeled as a delay of the purchase \
		rate (see Business Dynamics, Ch. 11, for details).  The average lifetime \
		of products is the Useful Life, but some products are retired sooner and \
		some later than the average.
	|

Initial Unit Cost=
	1900
	~	$/unit
	~	Initial unit costs.
	|

Unit Cost=
	Initial Unit Cost*Effect of Experience on Cost
	~	$/unit
	~	Unit costs are the initial unit costs modified by the effect of experience \
		(driven by the learning curve).
	|

Useful Life of Product=
	5
	~	Year [0,16,0.25]
	~	The lifetime over which the product will be useful.
	|

********************************************************
	.BassVCR
********************************************************~
		
		Bass Diffusion Model Modified to Represent VCRs
		John Sterman (2001) Instructor's Manual for Business Dynamics:
		Systems Thinking and Modeling for a Complex World.  
		Irwin/McGraw-Hill
		Copyright (c) 2001 John Sterman
		
		This is Bass Diffusion model modified to represent the diffusion of VCRs.
		The total size of the market depends on the product price, which falls
		according to a standard learning curve.  The installed base of VCRs
		is represented by an aging chain; discards generate replacement demand.
		See IM Chapter 9.
	|

Fraction Willing to Adopt=
	MAX(0,MIN(1,(Reference Price-Price)/Reference Price))
	~	Dimensionless
	~	The fraction willing to adopt as a function of price.  A linear demand \
		curve is assumed, bounded between [0, 1], and reaching zero when the price \
		equals the reference price.
	|

Total Population N= INTEG (
	Net Population Increase,
		Initial Population N0)
	~	Households
	~	The total number of households.
	|

Potential Adopters P=
	MAX(0, Population Willing to Adopt M - Adopters A)
	~	Households
	~	The potential adopter population is the total population willing to buy \
		the product, less those who already have, and constrained to be \
		nonnegative.
	|

Production Experience=
	Switch for Exogenous Purchases*Exogenous Purchase Rate + (1-Switch for Exogenous Purchases\
		)*Purchase Rate
	~	Units/Year
	~	Cumulative experience rises with the purchase rate for the product (assumes \
		production and purchases are roughly equal, ignoring capacity constraints, \
		backlogs and inventories).
		To enable partial model testing, Production Experience is set either to an \
		exogenous purchase rate or to the endogenous purchase rate determined in \
		the installed base sector.
	|

Adoption from Word of Mouth AW=
	Adoption Fraction i*Contacts with Adopters CA
	~	Households/Year
	~	The rate at which households decide to adopt the product as the result of \
		word of mouth and social contagion.  Given by the probability of adoption \
		given a contact between a potential and current adopter and the number of \
		such contacts per year.  The probability of adoption conditioned on a \
		contact is the (constant) adoption fraction, i.
	|

Cumulative Experience= INTEG (
	Production Experience,
		Initial Experience)
	~	Units
	~	Cumulative production experience, a proxy for the experience that leads to \
		cost reductions through learning.
	|

Average Price=
	Unit Cost*(1+Markup)
	~	$/unit
	~	Price is determined by unit costs and a fractional markup.
	|

Adoption Fraction=
	Adopters A/Total Population N
	~	Dimensionless
	~	The fraction of the population that has adopted the product.  Includes \
		those who have made the decision to buy but have not yet purchased, while \
		the data are for owners only.
	|

Learning Curve Strength=
	ln(1 - Cost Reduction per Doubling of Experience)/ln(2)
	~	Dimensionless
	~	The learning curve strength (exponent in the learning curve fornulation).
	|

Cost Reduction per Doubling of Experience=
	0.05
	~	Dimensionless [0,0.2,0.005]
	~	The fractional reduction in cost per doubling of cumulative experience.
	|

Initial Experience=
	400
	~	Units [0,5000,50]
	~	The initial experience level at the start of production, representing the \
		experience carried over from prototypes, prior related products, and pilot \
		plants.
	|

Markup=
	0.2
	~	Dimensionless [0,0.4,0.01]
	~	The markup applied to unit costs to yield the price, as a fraction of unit \
		costs.  The markup covers fixed costs and the normal profit margin.
	|

Effect of Experience on Cost=
	(Cumulative Experience/Initial Experience)^Learning Curve Strength
	~	Dimensionless
	~	Costs decline by a fixed fraction for every doubling of cumulative \
		experience, capturing learning by doing.
	|

Initial Population N0=
	7.1e+07
	~	Households
	~	The initial number of households.  71 million in the US in 1975.
	|

Reference Price=
	2300
	~	$/unit [2000,2500,1]
	~	The price at which the fraction of the population willing to buy the \
		product is zero.
	|

Adoption Rate AR=
	Adoption from Advertising AA + Adoption from Word of Mouth AW
	~	Households/Year
	~	The rate at which households decide to purchase the product.
	|

Advertising Effectiveness a=
	0.001
	~	1/Year [0,0.02,0.0001]
	~	The fraction of the population adopting each year as the result of \
		advertising.
	|

Adoption from Advertising AA=
	Advertising Effectiveness a*Potential Adopters P
	~	Households/Year
	~	Adoption resulting from exposure to advertising.  Proportional to the \
		potential adopter population.  The fraction of the potential adopters \
		adopting per year as a result of advertising is given by the effectiveness \
		of advertising.
	|

Contact Rate c=
	100
	~	1/Year
	~	The rate of contacts among members of the population.
	|

Adopters A= INTEG (
	Adoption Rate AR,
		Initial Adopters A0)
	~	Households
	~	The population that have adopted the product.
	|

Adoption Fraction i=
	0.005
	~	Dimensionless [0,0.02,0.0001]
	~	The probability of adoption given a word of mouth contact with an adopter.
	|

********************************************************
	.Control
********************************************************~
		Simulation Control Paramaters
	|

FINAL TIME  = 2000
	~	Year
	~	The final time for the simulation.
	|

INITIAL TIME  = 1975
	~	Year
	~	The initial time for the simulation.
	|

SAVEPER  = 
        TIME STEP 
	~	Year
	~	The frequency with which output is stored.
	|

TIME STEP  = 0.03125
	~	Year
	~	The time step for the simulation.
	|

